This is the tricky stuff!

match.call should return a FunctionCall  ("lang" in R-speak)

1. find the Function indicated by the definition argument (or
call.getFunction() if definition is NULL)
    -- see FunctionCall.findFunction -- it may need to be moved
somewhere else
2. call ClosureDispatcher.matchArguments to match call.getArguments()
to function.getFormals()
3. return FunctionCall.newCall( calll.getFunctions(),
matchedArguments )

I'm not sure about the exact semantics.. i'm not sure what expand dots
does or does not do...
Also, I've struggled to organize the code in ClosureDispatcher so if
you have any ideas on how to better refactor it, have at it!

Alex

FunctionCalls are a strange beast...
They have the storage structure of a PairList and
they are implemented in R-2.x as a PairList where the first element in
the linked list is the function (usually a Symbol which will evaluate
to a Function when the FunctionCall is evaluated)
and the subsequent elements are the arguments to be passed to the
function.

